185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (The AS-IS model represents the current physical Capstone C thesis submission process. After the supervisor approves the final report, the student group prepares the PDF and decides whether to print at a nearby local shop or travel to Nilkhet for lower printing costs. The group then submits the file to the printing shop, where two or three copies are printed and bound as black books.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (After printing, the students inspect the copies for missing pages, incorrect page order, poor image quality, colour problems, or binding defects. When an error is found, the affected pages or the entire copy must be reprinted and checked again, increasing waiting time, electricity use, cost, and paper waste. Once all copies are correct, the students collect the black books, return to the university, obtain any required signatures, and physically submit the copies to the supervisor, department, and library.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
187 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (The model highlights several weaknesses in the current process, including repeated printing, student travel, long waiting periods, reprinting, paper and binding waste, transport-related emissions, and the need for physical storage. It also shows that the same report is duplicated across several locations, making retrieval, preservation, and administrative handling less efficient.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
